A||||6||86SE||84-87 Fieros - Do the brakes lock up??||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||06-01-2000||02:18 AM||||I own a '86 V-6 Fiero. The brakes seem really bad.  The only time i can get the wheels to lock up is on snow.  On snow i have to pop it out of drive to stop of the back tires will keep on turning while the front tires slide.<P>No matter how hard I try i cannot get them to lock up on dry pavement (not that i would want to).  The front pads are new as of '97/10,000 miles, the rear are still pretty good, still a lot of pad left.<P>My Fiero requires a very long disance and a heavy foot to stop it.  I have an '81 pontiac phonenix with front disc and rear drum and it stops on a dime!!  The Fiero has 4 wheel disc and stops like $*!^.<P>Is this i problem will all 84-87 Fieros or just mine??||||reg||5
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||06-01-2000||03:16 AM||||When I was driving his 86 GT, Dave said he'd never got the brakes to lock.. so I figured I'd see how well the car stops.. so I stepped on the brake and locked the wheels instantly... car slid to a stop.<P>I guess it depends how much force you apply to the pedal....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||06-01-2000||03:32 AM||||How is the idle? fast? erratic?<BR>Does the brake pedal feel 'hard'?<BR>My nickel is on leaky vacuum booster system.  Cracked diaphram, inop check valve, or just leaky hose connections.<BR>Or did some previous owner actually disconnect and plug it?<P>Ever clean the calipers and rotors with WD40, sure does leave a nice shine and is rust resistant also. ||||reg||12
Z||000003||86SE||06-01-2000||03:51 AM||||I know the vaccume is working.  Its much much easier to push when the car is on as opposed to when the car is off.  I dont seem to be leaking any fluid, it has been full for a couple years.<P>The idle fluctuates from about 900-1200, it never stays still.||||reg||1
Z||000004||triker||06-01-2000||07:33 PM||||You might want to try a more aggressive set of pads on the back.  It's a cheap and easy fix with the downside being shorter rotor life, though rear rotors are fairly cheap too, compared to the fronts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shiner||06-02-2000||12:37 AM||||In either my '84 SC (auto) or my '87GT (getrag) I can lock the wheels up.  The fronts lock up first, and if I REALLY try, I can get the rears to lock up.  Even thought the Fiero's brakes do take a lot of effort, I like them because they are easy to modulate.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||06-02-2000||01:42 PM||||Check to see that the calipers are free to slide back and forth on the sliders.  If not then the pad is only contacting one side of the rotor.  The caliper needs to be free to center itself on the rotor as the brakes are applied and released.||||reg||1
